// Leaf of the let-annotation multimodule probe.
//
// Checked ALONE this file reports 3 errors: E001 for `var n: ST = 7` (an integer
// bound to a struct annotation) and two E004 for the arithmetic on it. Checked as
// an IMPORTED module it reports none of them, because the type annotation on a
// let/var statement does not survive the multimodule path — the binding takes the
// initializer's type instead, and when there is no initializer it takes unit.
//
// That second case is the one with consequences in the real tree: `var out: T`
// with no initializer is how several `*_new` constructors in elf/encode/reloc are
// written, and every field access on such a binding is reported as
// "no field <x> on type ()".

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// INSTRUMENTED RESULT (2026-08-02). A throwaway build printed, at every
// StmtLet/StmtVar site in the parser and in all four statement checkers,
// whether the type annotation was present. It refutes the heading above this
// block as originally written:
//
//   Stmt.ty ARRIVES. Both the single-module and the multimodule run print
//   "check-inplace var SOME". The annotation is not lost in transit, and only
//   the in-place checker runs in either case — no value-path checker fires at
//   all. What differs is what LOWERING that annotation returns.
//
// Follow-up probes, same binary, no rebuild:
//
//   var k: i64      -> "no field len on type i64"     in BOTH runs
//   var w: ST       -> "on type ST" single, "on type ()" multimodule
//   var v: ST = ST{..} -> "on type ST" in BOTH  (the literal supplies the type)
//   let r = mk()    -> "on type ST" single, NO DIAGNOSTIC AT ALL multimodule
//
// So it is not statement annotations. Built-in type names lower fine
// everywhere; NAMED type names do not lower in the multimodule path at all —
// the fourth line shows a function's declared return type failing the same way.
// The struct table itself is populated, which the third line proves: a struct
// literal resolves ST in exactly the run where the annotation cannot.
//
// The two suspects named in the previous commit are BOTH eliminated, and by
// the same observation: checker_collect_items_mut is a one-line delegation to
// checker_collect_items_inplace, and check_items_verdict_boot4 is a one-line
// delegation to check_items_verdict_boot4_with_module_path. Single and
// multimodule run the same collector and the same checker over the same AST.
// The divergence is therefore checker STATE at the moment
// checker_lower_named_type_with_args_mut runs, not a different code path.

pub struct ST {
    len: i64,
    last: i64,
}

// Loses its annotation when imported: `n` becomes i64, so `n + 1` type-checks.
pub fn probe_arith_on_struct_ann() -> i64 {
    var n: ST = 7
    n + 1
}

// The shape that matters. No initializer, so with the annotation gone the
// binding is unit and both field accesses fail with E012.
pub fn probe_uninit_annotated() -> ST {
    var out: ST
    out.len = 0
    out.last = 0
    out
}
